          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"tolstoy-war-and-peace-0c29fdb355707ad8\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.98,\"analysis\":\"Princess Marya directly avows a longstanding, explicitly fraternal love for Pierre. The utterance presents her affection as true; it neither undertakes a new relational commitment nor functions as a reflex-like emotional discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The phrase “as a brother” relationally positions Pierre, but here it qualifies the kind of affection asserted rather than enacting an undertaking.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Princess Marya directly addresses Pierre: “я вас давно знаю и люблю как брата” (“I have known you for a long time and love you as a brother”).\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"The avowal follows Pierre’s remorseful explanation and Princess Marya’s tender smile, affirming her warm, non-romantic regard for him. She immediately redirects the conversation to her concern for Andrei and gives Pierre no time to answer. This may make the avowal socially reassuring and facilitate her ensuing appeal, but it remains principally a sincere-seeming statement of established affection, not a promise or reflexive outburst.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Ведь я вас давно знаю и люблю как брата” (“I have known you for a long time and love you as a brother”).\",\"supports\":\"The first-person present-tense wording explicitly presents longstanding fraternal love as Princess Marya’s emotional state, strongly supporting T.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Княжна Марья молча посмотрела на него и нежно улыбнулась” (“Princess Marya looked at him in silence and smiled tenderly”).\",\"supports\":\"Her tender demeanor supports the sincere affectionate context of the avowal, while not independently establishing expressive/reflexive force.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"She asks about Andrei “поспешно, не давая ему времени сказать что-нибудь в ответ на ее ласковые слова” (“hurriedly, giving him no time to reply to her affectionate words”).\",\"supports\":\"The narration identifies the utterance as affectionate words but depicts a controlled conversational transition rather than a reflex-like discharge or an exchange of commitments.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Later narration says Princess Marya had been won over by Pierre’s gentle treatment of the pilgrims and looked at him radiantly.\",\"supports\":\"This supplies a concrete basis for her favorable feeling toward Pierre and reinforces the truth-conditional avowal reading.\",\"confidence\":0.94}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the core force as an avowal of affectionate mental state. The tender delivery, relational qualification, and conversational purpose are adequately handled as context; they create no residual pressure for E or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":\"The provided analytical translation was treated only as an aid; the classification is grounded in the Russian source context.\"}"
        }
